Push vs Pull Deployment Model

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring an important concept in deployment strategies: Push vs Pull Deployment Models. 🚀

Understanding these models helps explain why GitOps prefers the pull-based approach for secure and reliable deployments.

🔹 Push Model (CI/CD Approach)
 In the push model, the CI/CD pipeline directly pushes changes to the infrastructure or Kubernetes cluster.
Workflow example:
 Developer → Git → CI/CD Pipeline → Deployment to Cluster
Characteristics:
CI/CD tools perform the deployment
Pipeline needs direct access to the cluster
Credentials and permissions must be stored in the CI system
While effective, this model can introduce security risks if credentials are exposed.

🔹 Pull Model (GitOps Approach)
 In the pull model, the cluster itself pulls the configuration from the Git repository.
Workflow example:
 Developer → Git → GitOps Tool → Cluster pulls updates
Characteristics:
GitOps controller runs inside the cluster
It continuously checks Git for changes
The cluster updates itself automatically
This reduces the need for external systems to access the cluster directly.

🔹 Why Pull Model is More Secure
 The pull model improves security because:
CI tools do not require direct cluster credentials
The GitOps controller manages deployments internally
Access control and audit trails are easier to maintain

💡 Key Insight:
 GitOps uses the pull model to improve security, reliability, and infrastructure consistency.
